/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  flash11_acetic_T_sweep --- flash10's chemistry with the temperature MOVING.

  The reactive acetic/water flash (flash10_acetic_water_reactive) is an
  isothermal (T,P) flash: T is the SPECIFICATION, not a result.  This case
  sweeps that specification across the SUBSATURATED window, 328.15 ->
  368.15 K, so the student sees temperature act through BOTH van't Hoff
  legs at once:

    * gas-liquid dissolution (dH = -51.5 kJ/mol): K falls as T rises, so
      every equilibrium partial pressure -- and their sum p_eq_sum_atm --
      CLIMBS toward P;
    * vapour dimerisation (dH = -58.5 kJ/mol): K_dim falls FASTER, so the
      dimer share of the acid's vapour FALLS as T rises even while the
      absolute p_dimer_atm grows with the monomer that feeds it.

  The responses are the flash's reactive KPIs (pH, p_eq_sum_atm,
  p_mono_atm, p_dimer_atm, dimer_share) -- published by the unit, read by
  the sweep driver, one CSV row per temperature.  The sweep deliberately
  STOPS below saturation: a two-phase reactive flash with a dimerising
  vapour is REFUSED by name (see flash10's header), and this case never
  asks for it.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoSolve;
description   "Reactive acetic/water flash: T swept 328->368 K in the subsaturated window (p_eq climbs, dimer share falls, pH drifts)";

verbosity     2;          // the sweep prints one line per point; Newton detail off
